#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Launch Codex with a live draft forecast under the composer.

    tf-codex [any codex arguments]

Codex has a status line, but it is not one anything outside Codex can write to.
`tui.status_line` takes a list of *built-in* item names — model, reasoning,
directory, branch, context left, token totals — configured through
`/statusline`; anything else is answered with `Ignored invalid status line
item`, and there is no hook, plugin or config key that runs a command and shows
what it prints. Claude Code's `statusLine.command` has no counterpart here.

So the bar is painted from outside. The launcher gives Codex a screen one row
shorter than the terminal really is, clamps every scrolling region Codex sets
to that shorter screen, and keeps the last row for the forecast — the same
technique a status bar has always used, and the reason Codex can neither scroll
over the row nor draw on it. The line itself is rendered by `statusline.js`,
the very program Claude Code runs, so both CLIs show one bar built by one piece
of code:

    ◆ prompt 9 tokens in → est 12k tokens out  ·  worst case 30k tokens

Everything the launcher knows about the prompt comes from the keystrokes it is
already forwarding, and it is reduced to counts before it is written anywhere.
See `tf_wrap` for that, `tf_reserve` for the row, and `tf_bar` for the renderer.
"""

import os
import sys

sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
from tf_wrap import run  # noqa: E402

if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(
        run(
            program="tf-codex",
            child_env="TOKEN_FORECASTER_CODEX_BIN",
            child_default="codex",
            reserve_row=True,
        )
    )
